<p>Let's give the <code>/harness</code> you installed some real work. The key difference is that you <strong>"convey only the intent"</strong>.</p>

<h3>Not "write it for me" but "I would like to write it"</h3>
<p>Normally you instruct an AI, "write me a report". But to the harness you state <strong>only the intent</strong>. Then it composes an <strong>agent team</strong> that will resolve that intent.</p>
<pre><code>/harness I would like to research the 2026 strategic technology budget allocation
across all national science and technology fields and the post-PBS response strategy, and write a report.</code></pre>
<ul>
    <li>Enter <code>/harness</code>, autocomplete it with <strong>Tab</strong>, then append your request.</li>
    <li>When you run it, it proceeds with <strong>domain analysis → team composition</strong>.</li>
    <li>Along the way it asks you to choose between things like <strong>"build the full harness / build only the harness"</strong>.</li>
    <li>If you have data of your own you can hand it over along with the question. (For example, it handles analyzing a PDF of more than 1,000 pages well.)</li>
</ul>

<h3>Composition done → review → run</h3>
<p>When the harness composition is finished, it pauses for a moment. <strong>Review</strong> the agents that were created, and if there is no problem, run it.</p>
<pre><code><span class="c"># After the review, just tell it to proceed</span>
Go ahead</code></pre>
<p>At that moment the work <strong>leaves human hands</strong>, and the agent team carries research → report writing → QA all the way through <strong>in the order the orchestrator planned</strong>.</p>

<h3>Several tasks in parallel</h3>
<ul>
    <li>You can leave one task running, bring up <strong>another terminal window</strong>, and give it yet another job. (For example, researching "can the KOSPI go up to 10,000?")</li>
    <li>At a working developer's desk, <strong>several</strong> terminals are up like this, doing different jobs at the same time.</li>
    <li>With <strong>Shift + arrow keys</strong> you can look at what the currently spawned agents are doing. (An agent with color on it is working.)</li>
</ul>

<div class="note">
    <span class="nh">What is it used for</span>
    Data analysis, research, report writing, simulation, and so on &mdash; whatever it is, give it the <strong>intent</strong> and it composes an agent team to do that job.
</div>
